-- Q11408: State and Local Government [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 34 (par. 34-36), restricted net position includes resources constrained by external parties or by law. The state highway grant ($2,800,000) is restricted by the grantor, and debt service reserves ($1,400,000) are restricted by bond covenants, totaling $4,200,000.
Wrong (A): $5,150,000 incorrectly adds the assigned amount ($950,000) to restricted. Assigned resources reflect internal intent, not external constraints.
Wrong (C): $8,350,000 includes both committed ($3,200,000) and assigned ($950,000) amounts, which are internal constraints classified as unrestricted net position on government-wide statements.
Wrong (D): $2,800,000 omits the debt service reserves required by bond covenants, which are restricted by an external creditor.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11408;

-- Q11411: State and Local Government [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 87 and GASB 34 (par. 100-102), amortization of the right-of-use asset is an operating expense because it represents the cost of using the asset in operations. Interest on the lease liability is classified as nonoperating expense, consistent with other interest costs in proprietary funds.
Wrong (A): Interest expense is always classified as nonoperating in proprietary fund statements, even when the underlying lease supports operations.
Wrong (C): GASB 87 requires an asset-liability model, not rental expense treatment. The lease payment is split between liability reduction, interest, and amortization.
Wrong (D): Amortization of the right-of-use asset is an operating cost. Only the interest component is nonoperating.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11411;

-- Q11412: State and Local Government [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB standards, when encumbrances lapse at year-end, the outstanding encumbrance entry is reversed by debiting budgetary fund balance--reserve for encumbrances and crediting encumbrances for $245,000. Since goods have not been received, no expenditure or liability is recorded, and the purchase order is re-encumbered in the next year.
Wrong (A): No liability exists because the goods have not been received. Accruing an accounts payable would be premature.
Wrong (B): This entry would increase encumbrances rather than reverse them, which is the opposite of what lapsing treatment requires.
Wrong (C): Automatic carryforward describes non-lapsing treatment. Under lapsing policy, the encumbrance entry must be reversed at year-end.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11412;

-- Q11415: State and Local Government [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 33, fines are imposed nonexchange revenues. Under modified accrual, revenue is recognized when measurable and available. The city recognizes $410,000 collected plus $80,000 expected within 60 days, totaling $490,000. The $70,000 deemed uncollectible is excluded.
Wrong (A): $560,000 includes the full levy without considering collectibility or the availability criterion. The $70,000 uncollectible amount must be excluded.
Wrong (B): $410,000 omits the $80,000 expected within the availability period. Amounts collectible within 60 days meet the available criterion.
Wrong (D): $480,000 appears to subtract only the uncollectible $70,000 from the amount collectible within the period, ignoring that the $80,000 available amount should be included.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11415;

-- Q11420: State and Local Government [easy/L2]
