-- Q11455: State and Local Government [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 62 (par. 63-68), a change in depreciation method is classified as a change in accounting principle requiring retroactive application. The $340,000 cumulative effect is reported as a restatement of beginning net position for the earliest period presented.
Wrong (A): Current-year expense adjustment would be prospective treatment. GASB 62 requires retroactive restatement for changes in accounting principle.
Wrong (C): The cumulative effect is not a deferred outflow. It is a direct restatement of beginning net position reflecting the retroactive application.
Wrong (D): While FASB treats depreciation method changes as changes in estimate (prospective), GASB 62 classifies them as changes in accounting principle requiring retroactive restatement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11455;

-- Q11490: Special Purpose Frameworks [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C Section 800, a special purpose framework (SPF) was historically called an other comprehensive basis of accounting (OCBOA). The AICPA replaced the OCBOA terminology with SPF in the clarified auditing standards, but the concept remains the same.
Wrong (A): "Simplified reporting standard" is not a recognized term in professional accounting literature or auditing standards.
Wrong (C): "Nonauthoritative reporting framework" is a fabricated term. Cash basis and other SPFs are recognized frameworks under AU-C 800.
Wrong (D): "Alternative measurement basis" is not a recognized professional designation for special purpose frameworks.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11490;
